adata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $6$ and $2,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $6,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=6\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $9$ and $6,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $9,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=9\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "36"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choice