ure-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={9}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={9}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=9.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/2d1cefc0c789518d21279c5c49a18f80220a2aad80973f0e84fda0ddaf25493b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $100$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=10={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={10}^{2}=100,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=8={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={8}^{2}=64.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=100$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A{A}^{T}{v}_{1}"] ["math" "A{v}_{1}"] ["math" "A{v}_{2}"] ["math" "{v}_{1}"] ["math" "{v}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{v}_{1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "7{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}"] ["math" "{v}_{1}"] ["math" "{v}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathac