s of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13159d7d5c6860406c5296d68a4d24ed916db843734f36548e75b4a55bbef43c"} {"question" "q-139548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$7$ and $5$"] ["text" "$7$ and $\\sqrt{5}$"] ["text" "$7{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$"] ["text" "$\\sqrt{7}$ and $10$"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "q-139548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$7$ and $5$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/state.json" "file_sha256" "8db308feb568fa0c1c2b6c0d11e8647c4baf266f2cbe17415f677e15d5c71807"} {"question" "e-9907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/example-9907.json" "file_sha256" "cb7af61c677cdd271954f8680265eb39297f7ab3b66e607e5c08364ad5cda4da"} {"question" "e-9907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\}  = \\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/example-9907.json" "file_sha256" "cb7af61c677cdd271954f8680265eb39297f7ab3b66e607e5c08364ad5cda4da"} {"question" "e-9909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4,$ $\\sigma_2=2,$ and $\\sigma_3 = 0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/example-9909.json" "file_sha256" "bca10f6e4f2621eb78d52109a690f6d778628d0efe3f2c259833a3d70e6e2dc1"} {"question" "e-9909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4,$ the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/example-9909.json" "file_sha256" "bca10f6e4f2621eb78d52109a690f6d778628d0efe3f2c259833a3d70e6e2dc1"} {"question" "e-9908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA,$ find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/example-9908.json" "file_sha256" "a6e333c9559b837bdf78f320c71857e02c82e1985602f980d2d9481a1405d882"} {"question" "e-9908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_1|| = 8 = \\sigma_1,$ which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64,$ and\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_2|| = 6 = \\sigma_2,$ which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = 36$ of $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/example-9908.json" "file_sha256" "a6e333c9559b837bdf78f320c71857e02c82e1985602f980d2d9481a1405d882"}] "usage" {"e-9908" [] "q-139421" [] "e-9909" [] "q-139415" [] "e-9907" [] "q-139418" []} "basis" 2806 "retractions" [[17592186162286 :question/problem "A $2 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^{T}A$ are $5$, $3$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis."] [17592186162287 :question/problem "A $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{2}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $σ_{1} = 10$ and $σ_{2} = 2\\sqrt{5}$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse."] [17592186162289 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3131/Source/Images/q-139421.png)\n\nA $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{2}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^{T}A$, which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of $A^{T}A$?"] [17592186161842 :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis."] [17592186161842 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}$."] [17592186161844 :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$, $\\sigma_2=2$, and $\\sigma_3 = 0$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse."] [17592186161844 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A$. Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4$, the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4$."] [17592186161843 :question/problem "A $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA$, find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3131/Source/Images/ba65b415e5958ff6ec13493e6641c014.png)"] [17592186161843 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1$,\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_1 \\mid \\mid = 8 = \\sigma_1$, which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64$, and\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_2 \\mid \\mid = 6 = \\sigma_2$, which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36$.\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = 36\n$$\n\nof $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186159259 17592186159260 17592186159261 17592186159262 17592186159263 17592186159265 17592186159266 17592186159267 17592186159268 17592186159269 17592186159277 17592186159278 17592186159279 17592186159280 17592186159281] "immutable_fields" [[17592186159258 :answer-field/correct] [17592186159258 :answer-field/id] [17592186159258 :answer-field/key] [17592186159258 :answer-field/type] [17592186159264 :answer-field/correct] [17592186159264 :answer-field/id] [17592186159264 :answer-field/key] [17592186159264 :answer-field/type] [17592186159276 :answer-field/correct] [17592186159276 :answer-field/id] [17592186159276 :answer-field/key] [17592186159276 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
